; spatial-timing-budget-band.fk — the exchange rate between time and space, over this
; room's actually-measured organs.
;
; preludes: form-stdlib/core.fk observe/spatial-timing-budget.fk
;
; Fixtures measured on this host 2026-07-22 22:30-22:33 WITA: adb best-of-15 round trip
; 50 ms (so clock offset is knowable to +/-25 ms), HATI_WINDOW=3 for the mac ear, and the
; 34 ms media-session poll interval from 20:59.
;
; Verdict 1023 when, on Go / Rust / TypeScript / fkwu:
;     1  one millisecond of timing uncertainty is 343 mm of position uncertainty
;     2  placing a sound within one metre needs under 3 ms across every organ
;     4  the measured clock offset alone costs 8575 mm
;     8  the ear's 3 s window costs 1 029 000 mm — a kilometre
;    16  and the ear is the bottleneck, not the clock
;    32  so the reachable placement today is 1 029 000 mm
;    64  a one-metre placement is not reachable
;   128  it is short by a factor of 1029, which sizes the work honestly
;   256  and with the ear at a 10 ms window the clock offset becomes the next bottleneck
;   512  while one sample at the ear's own 16 kHz is already 21 mm — the resolution is
;        present in the recording and discarded by the summarising step, not absent
;
; The teaching: space is bought with time at a fixed rate and no downstream cleverness
; buys it back. Two things hide inside "time sync" and fail differently — OFFSET, the
; constant difference between clocks, which is measurable and correctable; and WINDOW, the
; granularity at which a sensor timestamps at all, which is the sensor's own quantum and
; which no clock discipline shrinks. The mac ear's 3000 ms is a window, not an offset.
; That is why bit 16 matters more than bit 4: the room's spatial ceiling is set by a config
; value (HATI_WINDOW=3), not by the network, and bit 256 says what becomes true once it moves.

(do
    (spatial-timing-budget-check))
